    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Nested Models and the F-test\"\nsubtitle: \"Testing a whole batch of coefficients at once\"\nauthor: \"Prof. Eric Friedlander\"\nfooter: \"[🔗 MATH 2025 - Fall 2026 - Schedule](https://ericfriedlander.github.io/math2025-fa26/)\"\nlogo: \"../images/logo.png\"\nformat:\n  revealjs:\n    theme: slides.scss\n    multiplex: false\n    transition: fade\n    slide-number: false\n    incremental: false\n    chalkboard: true\n    scrollable: true\nhtml-math-method:\n  method: mathjax\n  url: \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-mml-chtml.js\"\nexecute:\n  freeze: auto\n  echo: true\n  cache: false\nknitr:\n  opts_chunk:\n    R.options:\n    width: 200\nbibliography: references.bib\n---\n\n\n\n# AE 19 Part 4: discussion\n\n## Discussing AE 19 Part 4 {.smaller}\n\n::: appex\n📋 [AE 19 - Your Pilot Data: t-tests](https://ericfriedlander.github.io/math2025-fa26/ae/ae-19-pilot-t-tests.html)\n:::\n\nTalk with a neighbor about your pilot data:\n\n::: question\n- What null hypothesis does the p-value for your primary variable in `add_fit` test? (Remember: *holding the other variables in the model constant*.)\n- Did that p-value change between `simple_fit` and `add_fit`? Why? What question is each p-value answering?\n:::\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<countdown-timer class=\"countdown\" id=\"timer_8e477076\" minutes=\"5\" seconds=\"0\" update-every=\"1\" tabindex=\"0\" style=\"right:0;bottom:0;\"></countdown-timer>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Categorical predictors\n\n## Categorical predictors: tests against a reference level\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlm(Sales ~ Location, data = coffee) |> tidy() |> kable(digits = 3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term             | estimate| std.error| statistic| p.value|\n|:----------------|--------:|---------:|---------:|-------:|\n|(Intercept)      |   74.356|     2.950|    25.207|   0.000|\n|LocationCityHall |   -5.844|     4.172|    -1.401|   0.163|\n|LocationPark     |   11.511|     4.172|     2.759|   0.006|\n|LocationZoo      |  -19.067|     4.172|    -4.571|   0.000|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEach `Location` p-value compares that location with the **reference level** (Business), not with every other location.\n\n## Relevel your factors to get your desired reference level\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code  code-line-numbers=\"|2\"}\ncoffee_zoo_base <-  coffee |>\n  mutate(Location = relevel(factor(Location), ref = \"Zoo\"))\n\nlm(Sales ~ Location, data = coffee_zoo_base) |>\n  tidy() |> kable(digits = 3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term             | estimate| std.error| statistic| p.value|\n|:----------------|--------:|---------:|---------:|-------:|\n|(Intercept)      |   55.289|     2.950|    18.744|   0.000|\n|LocationBusiness |   19.067|     4.172|     4.571|   0.000|\n|LocationCityHall |   13.222|     4.172|     3.170|   0.002|\n|LocationPark     |   30.578|     4.172|     7.330|   0.000|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nYou might want to do this if you have an experiment with one control (reference level) and multiple treatments.\n\n# Review: t-tests in MLR\n\n## Where we are {.smaller}\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nmlr_fit <- lm(Sales ~ Temperature + Location + TimeOfDay, data = coffee)\nmlr_fit |> tidy() |> kable(digits = 3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term               | estimate| std.error| statistic| p.value|\n|:------------------|--------:|---------:|---------:|-------:|\n|(Intercept)        |   93.548|    16.396|     5.706|   0.000|\n|Temperature        |   -0.620|     0.274|    -2.262|   0.025|\n|LocationCityHall   |   -4.164|     3.314|    -1.256|   0.211|\n|LocationPark       |   11.484|     3.230|     3.556|   0.000|\n|LocationZoo        |  -17.634|     3.291|    -5.358|   0.000|\n|TimeOfDayLunch     |   25.447|     2.803|     9.080|   0.000|\n|TimeOfDayAfternoon |   26.206|     2.797|     9.370|   0.000|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEach row is a $t$-test of $H_0: \\beta_j = 0$ **given the other predictors**.\n\n::: question\n`Location` has three rows (`LocationCityHall`, `LocationPark`, `LocationZoo`). Each compares one location with the Business District. Is there a single p-value that answers \"does `Location` matter at all?\"\n:::\n\n# A correction: what do we shuffle?\n\n## Correction: shuffle the **response** {.smaller}\n\nLast time I said that, to test one coefficient, we shuffle the **explanatory** variable. **That was wrong.** We shuffle the **response**:\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlm(shuffle(Sales) ~ Temperature + TimeOfDay, data = coffee) # correct: shuffle the response\nlm(Sales ~ shuffle(Temperature) + TimeOfDay, data = coffee) # wrong: shuffle an explanatory variable\n```\n:::\n\n\n. . .\n\n- Shuffling `Sales` breaks the link between the response and **all** of the predictors, but the predictors stay exactly as we observed them, **including how they relate to each other**.\n- Shuffling `Temperature` also breaks the relationship between `Temperature` and `TimeOfDay`. The shuffled data sets have predictors that look nothing like ours.\n\n::: question\nWhy might it matter if the shuffled data sets have predictors that are related differently from the real ones?\n:::\n\n## Does it matter? {.smaller}\n\nA simulated data set where `X1` and `X2` are highly correlated ($r = 0.95$), and the true slope of `X1` is **0**:\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ncorr_fit <- lm(y ~ x1 + x2, data = corr_data)\nset.seed(2026)\nshuf_y <- mosaic::do(1000) * lm(mosaic::shuffle(y) ~ x1 + x2, data = corr_data)\nshuf_x <- mosaic::do(1000) * lm(y ~ mosaic::shuffle(x1) + x2, data = corr_data)\n\nbind_rows(tibble(method = \"Shuffle Y (correct)\", slope = shuf_y$x1),\n          tibble(method = \"Shuffle X1 (wrong)\", slope = shuf_x[[2]])) |>\n  gf_histogram(~ slope | method, bins = 30, fill = \"grey80\", color = \"white\") |>\n  gf_vline(xintercept = coef(corr_fit)[[\"x1\"]], color = \"#533860\", linewidth = 1.2) |>\n  gf_labs(x = \"X1 slope from a shuffled data set\", y = \"Number of shuffles\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](20-nested-f-test_files/figure-revealjs/shuffle-compare-1.png){fig-align='center' width=80%}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: question\nWhich null distribution is narrower? What would that do to the p-value? To see why, look at the next two slides.\n:::\n\n## Correlated predictors: a wobbly plane {.smaller}\n\n`X1` and `X2` are highly correlated ($r = 0.95$). The truth: `Y` depends on `X2` only (true `X1` slope = 0).\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div id=\"htmlwidget-730f0e8140ffc2f7c865\" style=\"width:80%;height:450px;\" class=\"plotly html-widget\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-730f0e8140ffc2f7c865\">{\"x\":{\"visdat\":{\"10d7e45f554008\":[\"function () \",\"plotlyVisDat\"]},\"cur_data\":\"10d7e45f554008\",\"attrs\":{\"10d7e45f554008\":{\"alpha_stroke\":1,\"sizes\":[10,100],\"spans\":[1,20],\"x\":[-0.81031547189302355,0.10332756866208691,-1.0462268133293786,1.6953993114427977,0.26783628206948396,-1.0291288179478124,0.44594292232327293,0.72890783739561826,0.54558833497803594,-0.44821174733770647,1.6012268277809114,0.33588377285499404,-0.80443589117862691,-2.6015697694257427,1.1649297176248625,-0.15446586779152416,-0.12204857259909603,0.96068764342291035,0.82240008403815534,0.56602438316620951,0.932651369328382,0.77831939273830353,-0.01969307009572693,-2.3474177698573477,0.59526244733372047,-0.16709195007295499,-0.27949803605334683,-1.7625315484865236,-0.64305566022997063,0.36757359975648768,1.4285558964061382,-0.21971484880407391,0.33343457281817368,-0.16447123796472268,-1.6568629863988311,-0.5718276888255186,-0.54847663418991199,-0.1706836673442238,1.1368407663898976,0.75693531929562119],\"y\":[-0.808823891