497803594,-0.44821174733770647,1.6012268277809114,0.33588377285499404,-0.80443589117862691,-2.6015697694257427,1.1649297176248625,-0.15446586779152416,-0.12204857259909603,0.96068764342291035,0.82240008403815534,0.56602438316620951,0.932651369328382,0.77831939273830353,-0.01969307009572693,-2.3474177698573477,0.59526244733372047,-0.16709195007295499,-0.27949803605334683,-1.7625315484865236,-0.64305566022997063,0.36757359975648768,1.4285558964061382,-0.21971484880407391,0.33343457281817368,-0.16447123796472268,-1.6568629863988311,-0.5718276888255186,-0.54847663418991199,-0.1706836673442238,1.1368407663898976,0.75693531929562119],\"x2\":[-0.12497744177064088,-0.43899076566632683,0.88666691226257088,0.084526306280947636,-0.96095047227831742,-0.67593621981888596,0.16597573541747218,0.54753277188333915,-0.38678168038974409,0.95001232908470346,-0.069224885355866017,-0.89184640928855308,0.43476440869088867,-0.77333210405768815,1.1821298607036705,2.1006692483742802,-0.51220334666352174,-1.5186922867137873,0.30485899294582586,-0.41680048786040458,2.311777683811207,-0.36052883892157706,0.63660489997085579,0.45110613260766252,-1.0986537322140875,0.11544328213789912,-2.0705804459736035,1.90849368409557,0.18823156058429852,2.19088154020185,0.057386262469098157,-0.86950733371306776,0.46565809850583029,-1.1312807726536065,-1.1339901591318353,0.32479377865303327,-0.49607402923655014,-0.091991914145754014,-0.31901945684234373,-0.96615070598484287],\"beta\":[2,0,1],\"sigma\":1.5,\"lim\":2.5}}]}}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: question\nCompare with the last slide. Why does the plane wobble so much less?\n:::\n\n## Why this matters for shuffling {.smaller}\n\n- When `X1` and `X2` are correlated, the data can't easily separate their effects: many different planes fit almost equally well. So $\\hat\\beta_1$ varies a lot from sample to sample, and its **standard error is large**.\n- Shuffling `X1` would make it uncorrelated with `X2`, turning the wobbly picture into the steady one. The null distribution would be **too narrow** and the p-values **too small**.\n- Shuffling `Y` keeps the correlation between the predictors, so the null distribution reflects the wobble we really have.\n\n**Shuffle the response.**\n\n# Testing Batches Of Coefficients\n\n## Is Location Important?\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlm(Sales ~ Temperature + Location + TimeOfDay, data = coffee) |> \n  tidy() |> \n  kable(digits = 4)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term               | estimate| std.error| statistic| p.value|\n|:------------------|--------:|---------:|---------:|-------:|\n|(Intercept)        |  93.5480|   16.3961|    5.7055|  0.0000|\n|Temperature        |  -0.6199|    0.2740|   -2.2622|  0.0249|\n|LocationCityHall   |  -4.1638|    3.3139|   -1.2565|  0.2106|\n|LocationPark       |  11.4836|    3.2295|    3.5558|  0.0005|\n|LocationZoo        | -17.6340|    3.2910|   -5.3582|  0.0000|\n|TimeOfDayLunch     |  25.4470|    2.8025|    9.0801|  0.0000|\n|TimeOfDayAfternoon |  26.2063|    2.7968|    9.3700|  0.0000|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n. . .\n\nDon't really have a way to determine whether the whole variable `Location` is important...\n\n## Nested models {.smaller}\n\nA $t$-test checks **one** coefficient. To test several coefficients at once (like all the `Location` dummies), we compare two models.\n\nModel A is **nested** in Model B if B contains every predictor/coefficient in A, plus more. Setting the extra coefficients in B to 0 gives you A.\n\n| This model | is nested in this model | The extra terms |\n|:---|:---|:---|\n| `Sales ~ Temperature + TimeOfDay` | `Sales ~ Temperature + Location + TimeOfDay` | 3 `Location` dummies |\n| `Sales ~ Location + TimeOfDay` | `Sales ~ Location * TimeOfDay` | the interaction terms |\n| `Sales ~ Temperature` | `Sales ~ Location` | none: **not nested** |\n\nWhen comparing models, we call the smaller model the **reduced model** and the larger model the **full model**.\n\n. . .\n\n::: question\n- Why is `Sales ~ Temperature` not nested in `Sales ~ Location`? \n- Name a model that `Sales ~ Temperature` **is** nested in.\n:::\n\n## The $F$-test {.smaller}\n\n- A full model will always result in a higher $R^2$ and a lower $SSE$\n- Question: Is the improvement to be worth the extra terms?\n\n. . .\n\nWe measure fit with the **sum of squared errors**, $SSE = \\sum (y_i - \\hat y_i)^2$:\n\n$$\nF = \\frac{(SSE_{\\text{reduced}} - SSE_{\\text{full}}) \\,/\\, (\\text{number of extra coefficients})}{SSE_{\\text{full}} \\,/\\, df_{\\text{full}}}\n$$\n\n- **Numerator:** the improvement in fit **per extra coefficient**.\n- **Denominator:** the leftover noise per residual degree of freedom, i.e. $\\hat\\sigma_\\epsilon^2$ of the full model.\n- If the extra terms do nothing, $F$ is near 1. A large $F$ means the full model fits much better than we would expect from noise.\n\n. . .\n\n$H_0$: all the extra coefficients are 0 (the reduced model is enough). \n\n$H_A$: at least one is not 0. \n\nThe p-value comes from an $F$ distribution with (extra coefficients, $df_{\\text{full}}$) degrees of freedom.\n\n## Using `anova()` {.smaller}\n\nGive `anova()` the **reduced model first**, then the **full model**.\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nreduced <- lm(Sales ~ Temperature + TimeOfDay, data = coffee)\nfull <- lm(Sales ~ Temperature + Location + TimeOfDay, data = coffee)\nanova(reduced, full) |> tidy() |> kable()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term                                       | df.residual|      rss| df|    sumsq| statistic| p.value|\n|:------------------------------------------|-----------:|--------:|--:|--------:|---------:|-------:|\n|Sales ~ Temperature + TimeOfDay            |         176| 59173.55| NA|       NA|        NA|      NA|\n|Sales ~ Temperature + Location + TimeOfDay |         173| 40597.64|  3| 18575.91|  26.38604|       0|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n| Column | Meaning |\n|:---|:---|\n| `df.residual`/`Res.Df` | residual degrees of freedom of each model |\n| `RSS` | the sum of squared errors ($SSE$) of each model. R labels it \"residual sum of squares\" |\n| `df` | number of extra coefficients in the full model |\n| `Sum of Sq` | $SSE_{\\text{reduced}} - SSE_{\\text{full}}$ |\n| `statistics` and `p.value`/`F` and `Pr(>F)` | the test statistic and its p-value |\n\n. . .\n\n::: question\nHow many extra coefficients does `full` have? Check that `Sum of Sq` equals the difference in the two `RSS` values.\n:::\n\n## One test for a whole variable {.smaller}\n\nDoes `Location` matter **at all**, after accounting for `Temperature` and `TimeOfDay`? That is the `anova(reduced, full)` comparison we just ran.\n\n- $H_0$: all `Location` coefficients are 0.\n- A small p-value says at least one location differs, but not which. For that, look at the individual $t$-tests (the reference-level comparisons).\n\n::: question\nWhat do you conclude about `Location`? What would you conclude if the p-value were 0.6?\n:::\n\n## F-Test = T-Test for One Coefficient {.smaller}\n\n:::: columns\n::: column\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nfull_model <- lm(Sales ~ Temperature + TimeOfDay, data = coffee)\nfull_model |> tidy() |> kable()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term               |   estimate|  std.error| statistic|   p.value|\n|:------------------|----------:|----------:|---------:|---------:|\n|(Intercept)        | 122.453439| 19.0300619|  6.434737| 0.0000000|\n|Temperature        |  -1.142914|  0.3136588| -3.643814| 0.0003533|\n|TimeOfDayLunch     |  25.107106|  3.3539049|  7.485932| 0.0000000|\n|TimeOfDayAfternoon |  26.197618|  3.3477066|  7.825542| 0.0000000|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n:::\n::: column\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nreduced_model <- lm(Sales~ TimeOfDay, data = coffee)\nanova(reduced_model, full_model) |> tidy() |> kable()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term                            | df.residual|      rss| df|    sumsq| statistic|   p.value|\n|:-------------------------------|-----------:|--------:|--:|--------:|---------:|---------:|\n|Sales ~ TimeOfDay               |         177| 63637.58| NA|       NA|        NA|        NA|\n|Sales ~ Temperature + TimeOfDay |         176| 59173.55|  1| 4464.033|  13.27738| 0.0003533|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n:::\n::::\n\nExactly the same!\n\n# P-values and interaction\n\n## Do we need the interaction? {.smaller}\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlm(Sales ~ Location * Temperature, data = bc) |> tidy() |> kable() # bc is just the business and city hall data\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term                         |    estimate|  std.error|  statistic|   p.value|\n|:----------------------------|-----------:|----------:|----------:|---------:|\n|(Intercept)                  |  87.2205966| 41.4288759|  2.1053093| 0.0381810|\n|LocationCityHall             | -10.6226164| 58.4856136| -0.1816279| 0.8563022|\n|Temperature                  |  -0.2190416|  0.7039482| -0.3111615| 0.7564308|\n|LocationCityHall:Temperature |   0.0874289|  0.9721659|  0.0899320| 0.9285503|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n- The interaction p-value tests: \"**does the effect of one variable depend on the other?**\"\n- Main-effect p-values become hard to interpret and can safely be ignored... Once interaction is important, both variables that are interacting must also be important.\n- Keep both main effects in the model whenever you keep their interaction.\n\n::: question\nInterpret this p-value in context. What would you tell Jo?\n:::\n\n## P-values in the interaction model {.smaller}\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nint_fit <- lm(Sales ~ Location * TimeOfDay, data = coffee)\nint_fit |> tidy() |> kable(digits = 3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term                                | estimate| std.error| statistic| p.value|\n|:-----------------------------------|--------:|---------:|---------:|-------:|\n|(Intercept)                         |   74.533|     3.154|    23.630|   0.000|\n|LocationCityHall                    |  -29.400|     4.461|    -6.591|   0.000|\n|LocationPark                        |   -3.600|     4.461|    -0.807|   0.421|\n|LocationZoo                         |  -50.533|     4.461|   -11.329|   0.000|\n|TimeOfDayLunch                      |   -3.267|     4.461|    -0.732|   0.465|\n|TimeOfDayAfternoon                  |    2.733|     4.461|     0.613|   0.541|\n|LocationCityHall:TimeOfDayLunch     |   47.667|     6.308|     7.556|   0.000|\n|LocationPark:TimeOfDayLunch         |   18.333|     6.308|     2.906|   0.004|\n|LocationZoo:TimeOfDayLunch          |   50.467|     6.308|     8.000|   0.000|\n|LocationCityHall:TimeOfDayAfternoon |   23.000|     6.308|     3.646|   0.000|\n|LocationPark:TimeOfDayAfternoon     |   27.000|     6.308|     4.280|   0.000|\n|LocationZoo:TimeOfDayAfternoon      |   43.933|     6.308|     6.964|   0.000|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: question\nThis case has MULTIPLE interactions terms for the same two variables... How can we test them all at once?\n:::\n\n. . .\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nadd_fit <- lm(Sales ~ Location + TimeOfDay, data = coffee)\nanova(add_fit, int_fit) |> tidy() |> kable()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term                         | df.residual|      rss| df|    sumsq| statistic| p.value|\n|:----------------------------|-----------:|--------:|--:|--------:|---------:|-------:|\n|Sales ~ Location + TimeOfDay |         174| 41798.59| NA|       NA|        NA|      NA|\n|Sales ~ Location * TimeOfDay |         168| 25070.80|  6| 16727.79|  18.68222|       0|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## \"Not significant\" is not \"no effect\" {.smaller}\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nset.seed(2026)\nsmall <- bc |> filter(TimeOfDay == \"Lunch\") |> group_by(Location) |> slice_sample(n = 4) |> ungroup()\nlm(Sales ~ Location, data = small) |> tidy() |> kable(digits = 3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term             | estimate| std.error| statistic| p.value|\n|:----------------|--------:|---------:|---------:|-------:|\n|(Intercept)      |    67.50|     6.689|    10.092|   0.000|\n|LocationCityHall |    22.75|     9.459|     2.405|   0.053|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe full Lunch data showed a difference of about 18 cups. With 4 days per location, the p-value is large.\n\n::: question\nDoes a large p-value mean City Hall and the Business District sell the same amount? What went wrong? (Hint: Lessons 14-16.)\n:::\n\n. . .\n\n**Caution**: Collecting more data because you didn't get the result you were hoping for is bad practice. It typically means you had a poorly designed study. If you only collect more data when you don't get a significant result, you are biasing your analyses.\n\n## Activity: AE 20 {.smaller}\n\n::: appex\n📋 [AE 20 - Your Pilot Data: Nested Models and F-tests](https://ericfriedlander.github.io/math2025-fa26/ae/ae-20-pilot-f-tests.html)\n:::\n\nPaste in your code from AE 19 (loading your data and fitting `simple_fit`, `add_fit`, and `int_fit`), then:\n\n- Decide which of your models are **nested**.\n- Use `anova()` to test whether your second variable matters, and whether the interaction matters.\n- Think about small samples and multiple tests.\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<countdown-timer class=\"countdown\" id=\"timer_f698c4c9\" minutes=\"30\" seconds=\"0\" update-every=\"1\" tabindex=\"0\" style=\"right:0;bottom:0;\"></countdown-timer>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Recap {.smaller}\n\n- Model A is **nested** in Model B if B has every term in A plus more.\n- The $F$-test compares the **sum of squared errors** of a reduced and a full model: is the improvement more than noise?\n- Use `anova(reduced, full)` to test a whole variable, or an interaction, at once.\n- With an interaction, main-effect p-values are conditional, and the interaction p-value asks whether the effect **depends on** the other variable.\n- Many tests mean many chances for false positives: name your primary hypothesis in advance.\n- A large p-value with a small sample is not evidence of no effect.\n\n## Coming next {.smaller}\n\nA p-value only tells us whether a coefficient could be 0. It does not tell us **how big** the effect is, or how precisely we have estimated it.\n\n::: question\nJo's data say City Hall sells about 18 more cups at Lunch. How sure are we about that number?\n:::\n\nNext time: **confidence intervals**.\n",
